<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carthage</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carthaginian, Carthaginians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carthage was a Phoenician city founded on the coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, traditionally in the ninth century BCE, which became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>. It dominated trade in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and fought three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which destroyed it in 146 BCE. Its trade networks reached into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, including contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carthage.jpg" alt="Carthage" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ELEL09, montage · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montage_ville_de_Carthage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Acts of the Scillitan Martyrs, about Christians tried at <b>Carthage</b> in 180, are the oldest dated Christian document written in Latin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Celtiberians served widely as mercenaries: in 211 BCE some 20,000 hired by Rome deserted Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio, contributing to the destruction of both Scipio brothers' armies, and about 4,000 fought for <b>Carthage</b> at the Battle of the Great Plains in 203 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Competition for sea routes brought the cities into alliance with <b>Carthage</b> against the Phocaean Greeks, who in the 6th century BCE were founding colonies on the coasts of Sardinia, Spain and Corsica.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty’s greatest test came in 1270, when Louis IX of France landed at <b>Carthage</b> on what became the Eighth Crusade; disease ravaged the camp, Louis died near Tunis in August, and Charles of Anjou negotiated a treaty in which al-Mustansir paid an indemnity and renewed tribute to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late third century BCE the lands west of <b>Carthage</b> were held by two Berber peoples, the Massylii in the east and the larger Masaesyli in the west, whose king Syphax ruled from Siga and later from Cirta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After destroying <b>Carthage</b>, the Roman Senate ordered the farming treatise of the Carthaginian writer Mago, in twenty-eight books, to be translated into Latin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
